Z={
1:"Commentators note ghora karma, “terrible action”: Arjuna still sees war as dreadful. Madhusūdana says his question is sincere, not argumentative. Many readers recognise the dilemma: if wisdom is higher than action, why must the wise still act? Chapter 3 answers it at length.",
2:"Commentators note that Arjuna admits his own confusion: “you seem to confuse my understanding”. A good student reports confusion honestly. Madhusūdana says the confusion arises because Kṛṣṇa praised knowledge and then urged action. The answer will show that the two serve one goal.",
3:"Commentators note niṣṭhā, “settled way”: a whole life is shaped by the path chosen. Some modern teachers compare the two to two kinds of temperament, the reflective and the active. Neither is better in itself; each suits different people.",
4:"Commentators note that siddhi, perfection, is not reached merely by giving up. Gandhi wrote that he found peace not by withdrawing from work but by changing his attitude to it. Many monks also say that outer renunciation is only a beginning.",
5:"Commentators note avaśaḥ, “helplessly”: the guṇas drive action even when we resist. Modern biology agrees that the body is never fully at rest; the heart beats, cells work, the brain is active even in sleep. The Gītā’s point is that we cannot opt out of action, only choose its spirit.",
6:"Commentators note vimūḍhātmā, “deluded self”: the pretender deceives himself before others. Many traditions warn against outward piety without inner change. The Tamil saint-poet Tiruvaḷḷuvar says that matted hair and ash do not make a sage; inner purity does.",
7:"Commentators note viśiṣyate, “excels”: the karma yogi excels the pretender. Madhusūdana says self-control with the mind, combined with active work, is the right balance for most people. A farmer, a nurse or a teacher who works without selfishness is practising this verse.",
8:"Commentators note jyāyaḥ, “better”: action is better than inaction for one not yet free. The famous proverb “an idle mind is the devil’s workshop” expresses a similar idea. Commentators add that action must be right action, niyata, not just busyness.",
9:"Commentators note that the Vedic idea of yajña was not only ritual but a cosmic principle of exchange. Modern ecological thinkers have found in this idea a basis for sustainable living: take from nature only with gratitude, and give back. Many Indian festivals still express thanks to nature, rivers, cattle and harvest.",
10:"Commentators note that sacrifice is called kāma-dhuk, the wish-fulfilling cow: it yields what is needed. Madhusūdana says this is not greed but the natural fruit of a cooperative world. The image of the cow is chosen because the cow gives milk freely to all.",
11:"Commentators note śreyaḥ param, “the highest good”, as the goal of mutual care. Some modern readers find in this verse a model for social harmony: teachers and students, farmers and city-dwellers, rulers and citizens nourish each other. When one side only takes, the system fails.",
12:"Commentators note stena, “thief”: a strong word for one who takes without giving. Indian ethics names five debts, ṛṇas, owed by every person: to the gods, the sages, the ancestors, humanity and other beings. Living without repaying them is called theft.",
13:"Commentators note that food cooked only for oneself is called agham, sin. The Sikh langar, community kitchens in temples and the custom of feeding guests before oneself all express this verse. Many families still set aside a portion of each meal for a cow, a crow or a beggar.",
14:"Commentators note parjanya, rain-cloud, as a Vedic god of rain. Ancient Indian society depended on the monsoon; a failed monsoon meant famine. The verse links human action to the rains through sacrifice. Modern readers sometimes read it as a reminder that human behaviour affects the environment.",
15:"Commentators note nityaṃ yajñe pratiṣṭhitam, “ever established in sacrifice”: the Divine is present wherever there is offering. Madhusūdana says every true sacrifice is a meeting with Brahman. Some teachers say this is why acts of service feel sacred.",
16:"Commentators note mogham, “in vain”: a life of taking without giving is wasted. Madhusūdana says such a person misses the purpose of human birth. Many readers find this verse a challenge to consumer culture, which encourages taking without thought of giving back.",
17:"Commentators note that the person of 3.17 is the same as the sthita-prajña of chapter 2. Madhusūdana says such a person no longer has obligations because desire is gone. Yet, as the following verses show, they still act for others. Ramana Maharshi, who had no worldly duties, spent his life receiving visitors and answering questions.",
18:"Commentators note artha-vyapāśraya, “dependence for any purpose”: the free person depends on no one. Madhusūdana says this is not pride but freedom from need. Saints who own nothing often show this freedom: they neither flatter nor fear anyone.",
19:"Commentators note satatam, “always”: unattached action is a constant practice, not an occasional one. Gandhi, who read this chapter often, called the attitude anāsakti, non-attachment, and made it the title of his Gītā translation. He said it was the key to his life.",
20:"Commentators note sampaśyan, “seeing clearly”: the wise act after seeing what the world needs. Madhusūdana says loka-saṅgraha means preventing people from straying from the right path. Many leaders in Indian history, such as the emperor Aśoka after his conversion, tried to rule for the welfare of all.",
21:"Commentators note pramāṇa, “standard”: the leader sets the measure others follow. Teachers often quote this verse to parents: children learn more from what they see than from what they hear. The same applies to teachers, managers and public figures.",
22:"Commentators note triṣu lokeṣu, “in the three worlds”: even across all realms, the Lord has nothing to gain. Madhusūdana says the Lord acts only for the world’s good. In the Udyoga Parva he goes himself as a messenger to the Kaurava court, a task usually given to a minister, to try for peace.",
23:"Commentators note atandritaḥ, “untiring”: the Lord’s work never stops. The sun rising daily, the seasons turning and the heart beating are often given as examples. Many find in this verse an encouragement to steady, quiet work.",
24:"Commentators note upahanyām, “I would destroy”: inaction by those responsible destroys others. Madhusūdana says the Lord, by acting, protects the order of the world. The same applies on a small scale: when a parent, teacher or leader stops doing their part, those who depend on them suffer.",
25:"Commentators note that the wise should work as hard as the unwise, but with a different motive. Vinoba Bhave walked thousands of miles across India in the Bhūdān movement, asking landowners to give land to the landless, as an example of tireless, selfless work.",
26:"Commentators note joṣayet, “let him encourage”: the wise should encourage others in their work, not belittle it. Madhusūdana says teaching beyond a person’s level confuses them. Good teachers meet students where they are.",
27:"Commentators note kartā, “doer”: the ego claims to be the sole author of actions. Some modern neuroscience suggests that many actions begin before we are conscious of deciding them. The Gītā’s point is spiritual, but the humility it teaches is supported by such findings.",
28:"Commentators note guṇa-karma-vibhāga, “the division of guṇas and actions”: the wise understand how nature works. Madhusūdana says this knowledge frees one from attachment. A doctor understands disease without being frightened by it; the wise understand the mind without being ruled by it.",
29:"Commentators note mandān, “the slow”: those not yet ready for deep teaching. Madhusūdana says the wise should not shake their faith. The verse shows the Gītā’s respect for every stage of the spiritual path.",
30:"Commentators note adhyātma-cetasā, “with the mind on the Self”: offering is joined to inner awareness. Madhusūdana says the fever of anxiety, jvara, is cured by offering. Many who face stressful work find that dedicating it to something higher brings calm.",
31:"Commentators note nityam, “constantly”: following the teaching is a daily practice. Madhusūdana says even those not yet perfect are freed by faithful practice. The verse encourages ordinary people who try sincerely.",
32:"Commentators note acetasaḥ, “senseless”: those who reject the teaching out of malice lose their own good. Madhusūdana says they harm only themselves. The Gītā does not threaten; it describes the natural result of closing one’s mind.",
33:"Commentators note jñānavān api, “even the wise”: everyone acts according to nature. Madhusūdana says even the learned are moved by past impressions. The verse is realistic: change of nature is slow, and forcing it does not work.",
34:"Commentators note vyavasthitau, “set” or “fixed”: likes and dislikes are fixed in the senses. Madhusūdana says the remedy is not to obey them. A practical tip many teachers give: when a strong like or dislike arises, wait before acting on it.",
35:"Commentators note bhayāvahaḥ, “fraught with fear”: another’s path brings danger. Madhusūdana says one acting against one’s nature is always anxious. Career counsellors often give similar advice: work that fits your strengths brings both success and peace.",
36:"Commentators note balād iva niyojitaḥ, “as if forced”: wrongdoing feels compelled. The Mahābhārata shows many characters who knew right from wrong but could not act on it, such as Dhṛtarāṣṭra, who loved his son too much to restrain him.",
37:"Commentators note mahā-pāpmā, “greatly sinful”: desire leads to many wrongs. Madhusūdana says desire and anger are one force in two forms. Many teachers advise studying one’s own desire closely, since it is the root of anger.",
38:"Commentators note āvṛtam, “covered”: knowledge is not destroyed by desire, only hidden. This gives hope: the Self’s clarity is always there, waiting to be uncovered. The three images show different degrees of effort needed.",
39:"Commentators note jñānino nitya-vairiṇā, “the eternal enemy of the wise”: even the wise must stay alert. Madhusūdana says the ignorant see desire as a friend because it promises pleasure. The wise see it as an enemy because it brings pain.",
40:"Commentators note adhiṣṭhāna, “seat” or “base”: desire has strongholds. Madhusūdana says one must attack it in its seats. Military imagery fits Arjuna, a warrior: know the enemy’s bases before battle.",
41:"Commentators note ādau, “first”: begin with the senses. Madhusūdana says controlling the senses weakens desire’s supply lines. Simple practices like fasting on certain days, keeping silence or limiting entertainment train the senses.",
42:"Commentators note that this ladder shows the order of control: what is higher can govern what is lower. Madhusūdana says the Self, being highest, can govern all. Many meditation teachers use this order to guide practice: senses, mind, intellect, then rest in the Self.",
43:"Commentators note saṃstabhya, “steadying”: the Self is used to steady the mind. Madhusūdana says the victory over desire is the greatest victory. The chapter that began with Arjuna’s confusion about action ends with a call to inner battle.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
